                    },
                    {
                      "type": "frame",
                      "id": "As8PG",
                      "name": "cmp/ChatBrowse",
                      "context": "ChatBrowse — the conversation scrubber in the ChatPanel's left gutter. One tick per user message, in order, so a long session becomes something you can scan and jump through instead of scrolling. It is the only navigation the turn log has. It indexes the whole conversation, not the visible fragment, so the tick count does not change as the log scrolls.\n\nThe rail is a lens, not a chart: tick length encodes distance from the pointer, never message content. At rest it is a uniform 10-wide ladder; the ticks nearest the cursor swell toward 26 (raised-cosine over 4 neighbours), which makes a 2px target tractable without moving it — growth is along X only, so no tick ever changes y. The tick under the pointer is tick='active' and raises cmp/ChatBrowseCard with that turn; clicking scrolls the log to that message. The rail is pinned to the viewport, centred vertically — it does not scroll with the log.\n\nTwo modes, by container width:\n• ≥480 — gutter. In-flow beside the turn log, width 40 (px 7, so a 26px tick clears exactly), gap 7. It reserves its own space and can never cover a message. The empty gutter right of a resting tick is the room the lens needs; do not shrink the frame to the ink, it IS the hit target.\n• <480 — floating. A 40px gutter is affordable in a full view and not in a sidebar, so the rail stops reserving space and overlays the log's left edge: the messages keep the full width and gain no padding. It fades in on pointer proximity rather than sitting there permanently.\n\nSCRIM (floating only): the rail is the one thing here that covers content, so it needs a background — but that background must be the surface it covers. $rail-float-fill is the panel's own bg at full alpha, held to 55% of the width and then fading to $rail-float-fade. Do NOT reach for bg.subtle or an elevated tone: anything lighter than the panel stops reading as the panel showing through and becomes a grey chip pinned to the messages. Skip Pencil's background_blur — it previews as a light frosted plate and misrepresents the colour.\n\nBALANCE: in gutter mode the rail is an inset, not a margin — mirror it. The log gets an equal 40 on the right, and the composer takes the same inset on both sides, so the turn log and the input area share one column (panel padding 16 + 40 = 56 a side) and the conversation stays centred. A rail that pads only the left is the bug this rule exists to prevent. Floating mode needs no mirror: it reserves nothing, which is the whole point.\n\nNever place the rail beside the composer in either mode. Hide it below ~3 user messages, where it costs a gutter and buys nothing.",
                      "reusable": true,
                      "width": 40,
                      "layout": "vertical",
                      "gap": 7,
                      "padding": [
                        10,
                        7
                      ],
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "id": "c0UiQ",
                          "type": "ref",
                          "ref": "qF9a4",
                          "name": "Tick 1",
                          "width": 10,
                          "theme": {
                            "tick": "default"
                          }
                        },
                        {
                          "id": "h6FFlB",
                          "type": "ref",
